---
/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * TEMPLATE — data / analytics case study.
 *
 * Astro ignores files starting with "_", so this never becomes a page.
 * To use it:
 *   1. Copy to src/pages/work/<your-slug>.astro
 *   2. Add the matching entry to src/data/projects.ts with discipline: 'data'
 *   3. Change getProject('...') below to your slug
 *
 * The section order is deliberately different from the search case studies.
 * A data project is judged on whether the reasoning holds up, so the structure
 * is: what was asked -> what data -> how -> what was found -> what it does NOT
 * support. The Limitations section is not optional; leaving it out is the most
 * common way a portfolio analysis reads as junior.
 * ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
import { getProject } from '../../data/projects';

const project = getProject('REPLACE-WITH-SLUG');

const sections = [
  { id: 'question', label: 'The question' },
  { id: 'data', label: 'The data' },
  { id: 'method', label: 'Method' },
  { id: 'analysis', label: 'Analysis' },
  { id: 'findings', label: 'Findings' },
  { id: 'limitations', label: 'Limitations' },
  { id: 'reproduce', label: 'Reproduce it' },
  { id: 'why-it-matters', label: 'Why it matters' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout project={project} sections={sections}>
  <section class="cs-section" id="question">
    <h2>The question</h2>
    <p>
      State the decision this analysis was meant to inform, in one short paragraph.
      Not "I analyzed the dataset" — what would someone do differently depending on
      the answer?
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="data">
    <h2>The data</h2>
    <p>Where it came from, how much of it, and what shape it's in.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Source:</strong> name it and link it if public</li>
      <li><strong>Period:</strong> date range covered</li>
      <li><strong>Grain:</strong> what one row represents</li>
      <li><strong>Size:</strong> rows and columns</li>
      <li><strong>Known issues:</strong> missing values, duplicates, inconsistent categories</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Say what you had to clean and why. Cleaning decisions are analysis decisions —
      showing them is more convincing than a tidy result that appeared from nowhere.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="method">
    <h2>Method</h2>
    <p>
      The approach and why it fits the question. Name the techniques plainly
      (aggregation, cohort comparison, regression, time series) and say what you
      considered and rejected.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="analysis">
    <h2>Analysis</h2>
    <p>Walk through the steps. Include the actual query where it carries weight:</p>
    <pre style="overflow-x:auto;background:var(--c-sand);border:1px solid var(--c-rule);border-radius:3px;padding:1rem;font-size:0.85rem;"><code>-- Example: keep snippets short and load-bearing.
SELECT category, COUNT(*) AS n, AVG(value) AS avg_value
FROM observations
WHERE observed_at >= '2026-01-01'
GROUP BY category
ORDER BY n DESC;</code></pre>
    <p>
      Charts go here as figures. Use the ImagePlaceholder component until the real
      export exists — see the DCHP case study for the pattern.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="findings">
    <h2>Findings</h2>
    <p>Lead with the answer to the question, then support it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Finding, with the number that supports it</li>
      <li>Finding, with the number that supports it</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      State effect sizes, not just direction. "Churn was 3.2 points higher" beats
      "churn was higher."
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="limitations">
    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      What this analysis does not establish. Confounders, sample limits, the
      correlation you are not claiming is causal, the period that may not generalize.
      This section is what separates an analyst from someone who makes charts.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="reproduce">
    <h2>Reproduce it</h2>
    <p>
      Link the repository and say what's in it: queries, notebook, data dictionary,
      how to run it. Set <code>repoUrl</code> in projects.ts and it also appears in
      the sidebar.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cs-section" id="why-it-matters">
    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <p>What this shows about how you work. Keep it to a short paragraph.</p>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
